sar means System Activity Reporter.

In simple language:

sar is used to monitor and check the performance/history of a Linux system.

It can give you information about CPU, memory, disk, network, load, etc.

Think of it as a system performance report.

1. Check CPU usage

sar

or:

sar -u

Example:

12:00:01  CPU  %user  %system  %idle
12:10:01  all   10.2     3.1    86.7
12:20:01  all   15.5     4.2    80.3

Important columns:

%user → CPU used by user applications
%system → CPU used by kernel/system
%idle → CPU that is free

If %idle is very low, CPU usage is high.

2. Check memory usage

sar -r

It gives information about RAM and memory usage.

Example:

kbmemfree   kbmemused   %memused
  2000000     6000000       75.0

So approximately 75% memory is being used.

3. Check disk activity

sar -d

This helps you see disk/device activity.

For example, if an application is slow, you can investigate whether the disk is heavily loaded.

4. Check network activity

sar -n DEV

This shows network traffic for interfaces such as:

eth0
ens33
enp0s3

You can see things like packets received/sent and network throughput.

5. Why is sar useful for a Linux administrator?

Suppose a user says:

"The server was very slow at 10 AM."

If you only run:

top

you see current activity.

But sar can help you look at historical performance data, provided sysstat collection is configured and running.

You can investigate things like:

CPU usage
Memory usage
Disk activity
Network activity

6. Lab Example

a) First check whether sar is installed:

sar -V

b) If it isn't installed on Ubuntu:

sudo apt install sysstat

c) Then enable the data collection service:

sudo systemctl enable --now sysstat

=> Now try:

sar -u

7. You may see this directory:

/var/log/sa

This is commonly used by sysstat, a Linux system monitoring package.

It stores historical performance data.

Think:

/var/log/sa = A place where Linux performance statistics are stored.

For example:

CPU usage
Memory usage
Disk activity
Network activity
System load

These statistics are useful when troubleshooting.

8. Why is /var/log/sa useful?

Suppose a server became slow yesterday.

You were not watching it at that exact time.

How can you investigate?

Historical monitoring data can help.

For example:

Yesterday at 10:00 AM
CPU usage was high

or:

Yesterday at 11:00 AM
Memory usage increased

This helps you understand what happened.

9. What is sar?

The command commonly used to read these statistics is:

sar

SAR = System Activity Report

It is part of the sysstat package.

You can install it on Ubuntu:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install sysstat

Then check:

sar

You may see CPU statistics.

10. What is stored in /var/log/sa?

You may see files such as:

sa08
sa09
sa10

These are commonly named using the day of the month.

For example:

/var/log/sa/sa08

may contain activity data for the 8th day of the month.

And:

/var/log/sa/sa09

may contain activity data for the 9th.


